<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inferno (Dante)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inferno is the first part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy</a>, written in the early 14th century. It describes Dante's journey, guided by the Roman poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, through the nine circles of Hell, where he meets sinners from history and from the Italian politics of his day. Among its best-known episodes is the story of the lovers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesca-da-rimini">Francesca da Rimini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-malatesta">Paolo Malatesta</a> in the fifth canto.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inferno-dante.jpg" alt="Inferno (Dante)" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inferno.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> probably began work on the Comedy around 1306, although some modern scholars argue that the composition of the Inferno began as late as 1313, and early commentators proposed start dates before 1302. He had been exiled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1302 after his faction, the pro-empire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-and-white-guelphs">White Guelphs</a>, was defeated by the pro-papal Black Guelphs, and he spent nearly all of the last fifteen years of his life on the Comedy. Many scholars agree that the Inferno was begun in 1306–1307 and completed in 1309–1310, though revisions probably continued. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a> and some other early commentators held that Dante composed the first seven cantos before his exile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The poem describes the journey of a fictionalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> through Hell, guided by the ancient Roman poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>. Hell is depicted as nine concentric circles of torment within the Earth, culminating at the centre, where Satan is held in bondage. As an allegory, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy</a> represents the journey of the soul toward God, and the Inferno the recognition and rejection of sin. Each punishment is a contrapasso, a symbolic instance of poetic justice: fortune-tellers, for example, must walk with their heads on backward because they tried to see the future by forbidden means.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem begins on the night of Maundy Thursday in 1300, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>, aged 35 and &quot;midway in the journey of our life&quot;, finds himself lost in a dark wood. His way is blocked by three beasts, a leopard, a lion and a she-wolf. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> explains that he has been sent by Beatrice, and the two enter the gate of Hell, which bears the inscription ending &quot;Abandon all hope, ye who enter here&quot;.</p><p>The circles are Limbo, for the unbaptised and the virtuous pagans; lust; gluttony; greed; wrath; heresy; violence; fraud; and treachery. The eighth circle, Malebolge, is a funnel of stone with ten concentric ditches, and the ninth is a frozen lake, Cocytus, where traitors are held in ice according to their guilt. Among the damned are historical persons and figures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>'s own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, such as the Ghibelline leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farinata-degli-uberti">Farinata degli Uberti</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, both in flaming tombs among the heretics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inferno (Dante)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Guido's daughter Francesca married into the Malatesta of Rimini and became the tragic heroine of <b>Dante's Inferno</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-ravenna">Lordship of Ravenna</a></li><li><blockquote>Malatesta's son Gianciotto killed his wife Francesca da Polenta and his brother Paolo, a story Dante told in the <b>Inferno</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-rimini">Lordship of Rimini</a></li></ul></section>
